From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 16:11:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] WIP: target/mips: the syscall trap

The syscall trap (system/syscall-trap.c) on MIPS, for o32, n32 and n64.

Entry: the syscall exception from user mode, outside a delay slot,
before it is delivered (mips_cpu_do_interrupt).  The ABI is the number's
range, as the kernel dispatches it: 4000-4999 o32, 5000-5999 n64,
6000-6999 n32; a 32-bit target only has o32.  o32 arguments 5 and 6
are read from the user stack (sp + 16, sp + 20) only when the number is
watched (syscall_trap_wants()).  The return address is the syscall
instruction + 4, where Linux returns.

Return: eret and eretnc back to user mode (exception_return), behind
syscall_trap_waiting().  The value is v0, negated when a3 is set; the
target writes v0 and a3 back for a changed return.

Task key: Linux's kernelsp[cpu], the current task's kernel stack top,
read from RAM at entry and at return.  Its address is found once per
vCPU by interpreting the kernel's own exception path from the general
exception vector (except_vec3_generic, SAVE_SOME) up to its load of the
stack pointer.  If that fails, the key is the ASID (or MMID).  The user
stack pointer is the second key word.

Fault, inject, rewind and restore as on the other targets.  o32 stack
arguments written by an injection or put back by a rewind fall back to
the RAM address seen at the entry when the stack page has left the
software-managed TLB.

The plugin ABI enum gains MIPS_N32 and MIPS_N64.  system/meson.build
builds the overlay's syscall-trap-selftest.c, a test consumer enabled by
SYSCALL_TRAP_SELFTEST.

